    "description": "The Orders application lets a planner revise an order for up to 45 minutes. One workspace can change the order header, line items, discounts, and shipping address, and those pieces must never be accepted as a partially updated cluster. The Order Service stores child records in separate tables, while clients may edit through the desktop app, a partner API, or a batch import. A revision stamp currently exists only on the header, so an address can change while an outdated line-item form is still open. Callers would have to discover and verify every affected child, which is error-prone and does not protect changes made by another workflow. The concurrency decision must cover the order and all of its members as one consistency boundary, without holding database locks for the full 45-minute editing period. A guard attached to the order root should therefore be checked whenever any member changes, while unrelated customer records remain independently editable.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose one shared concurrency guard for the order header and every line, discount, and address it contains, rather than requiring callers to coordinate each child separately.",
    "notes": "One root-level guard covers the order cluster so callers lock a single boundary instead of every child; the pessimistic and optimistic offline siblings stay in play as the guard's possible enforcement modes."
  },
